According to the Appellate Division in *Bangladesh & ors Vs. BLAST & ors*, 8 SCOB [2016] AD 1, a law enforcement officer who arrests a person must, as soon as practicable but not later than twelve (12) hours after the arrest:  

- Intimate a nearest relative of the arrestee;  
- In the absence of a relative, inform a friend suggested by the arrestee;  
- Notify them of the time and place of arrest and the location where the arrestee is being held.  

Additionally, an entry must be made in the diary recording:  
- The ground of arrest;  
- The name and address of the person who informed the police or made the complaint;  
- The names and particulars of the relative or friend notified about the arrest;  
- The particulars of the law enforcement officer in whose custody the arrestee is held.  

In this case, since no information was provided to the family within 12 hours of the arrest on 10 May 2026, and the arrestee has not been produced before a magistrate or given a case number, these procedural safeguards under the Appellate Division’s judgment have been violated.

According to the Appellate Division of the Supreme Court of Bangladesh in *Bangladesh & ors Vs. BLAST & ors*, 8 SCOB [2016] AD 1, a law enforcement officer who arrests a person must:

- Intimate a nearest relative of the arrestee, and in the absence of any relative, to a friend suggested by the arrestee, as soon as practicable but not later than twelve (12) hours after the arrest;  
- Notify the time and place of the arrest and the place where the arrestee is being held in custody;  
- Make an entry in the diary recording the grounds of arrest, the name and address of the person who informed the officer or made the complaint;  
- Disclose the names and particulars of the relative or friend to whom information about the arrest was given; and  
- Disclose their own identity and, if demanded, show their identity card to the arrestee and persons present at the time of arrest.

In this case, since no notification was given to the family within 12 hours and no details were provided regarding the arrest or custody, these guidelines have been violated.

According to the Appellate Division in *Bangladesh & ors Vs. BLAST & ors*, 8 SCOB [2016] AD 1, the police officer who arrests a person must:

(i) Prepare a memorandum of arrest at the time of arrest and obtain the signature of the arrestee with the date and time of arrest;  
(ii) Intimate a nearest relative of the arrestee, or in their absence, a friend suggested by the arrestee, as soon as practicable but not later than six hours after the arrest, notifying the time, place of arrest, and place of custody;  
(iii) Make an entry in the diary recording the grounds of arrest, the name and address of the person who informed the police or made the complaint, and disclose the names and particulars of the relative or friend to whom information was given;  
(iv) Send copies of all documents—including the memorandum of arrest, a copy of the information or complaint relating to the cognizable offence, and entries in the diary—to the Magistrate at the time of production of the arrestee under Section 167 of the Code of Criminal Procedure.

Failure to comply with these requirements violates constitutional and procedural safeguards established by the Supreme Court. In this case, the police did not inform the family within six hours, nor has the arrestee been produced before a Magistrate despite over three days of detention—both constitute clear breaches of the guidelines laid down in *Bangladesh & ors Vs. BLAST & ors*.

According to the Appellate Division of the Supreme Court of Bangladesh in *Bangladesh & ors Vs. BLAST & ors*, 8 SCOB [2016] AD 1, when a police officer arrests a person, they are required to:

- Immediately inform, as soon as practicable but not later than 12 hours after the arrest, a nearest relative of the arrestee;  
- If no relative is available, then inform a friend suggested by the arrestee;  
- Notify them of the time and place of arrest, and the location where the person is being held in custody.

Additionally, an entry must be made in the police diary recording:
- The reason for the arrest;
- The name and address of the person who reported or complained about the arrest;
- The names and details of the relative or friend informed about the arrest;  
- The identity and particulars of the police officer holding the arrestee.

In your case, your brother was arrested on 10 May 2026 at around 9:00 PM, but your family was not told where he was taken until the next afternoon — more than 12 hours later. Also, no information has been given about a case number or formal charges.

This delay and lack of communication violates the clear guidelines laid down by the Appellate Division in *Bangladesh & ors Vs. BLAST & ors*. The police are legally required to inform your family within 12 hours — not wait until the next day, and certainly not leave you guessing for days without any official update.

You have the right to demand immediate production of your brother before a Magistrate under Section 54 and Section 167(2) of the Code of Criminal Procedure, as failure to do so within 24 hours (excluding travel time) makes the detention illegal. You may also file a complaint with the High Court Division for habeas corpus if he is not produced soon.

According to the Appellate Division of the Supreme Court of Bangladesh in *Bangladesh & ors Vs. BLAST & ors*, 8 SCOB [2016] AD 1, a law enforcement officer who arrests a person is required to:

(i) Intimate a nearest relative of the arrestee as soon as practicable, but not later than twelve (12) hours after the arrest;  
(ii) In the absence of a relative, inform a friend suggested by the arrestee;  
(iii) Notify the time and place of arrest and the place where the arrestee is being held in custody;  
(iv) Make an entry in the diary recording:  
 - The ground of arrest;  
 - The name and address of the person who informed the police officer or made the complaint;  
 - The names and particulars of the relative or friend to whom information about the arrest was given; and  
 - The particulars of the law enforcing officer in whose custody the arrestee is being held.

In the present case, as per the letter dated 14 May 2026, Md. Asif Hossain was arrested on 10 May 2026 at approximately 9:00 PM from his residence without a warrant, and no information was provided to the family until the next afternoon — well beyond the 12-hour limit mandated by law. Furthermore, no case number has been disclosed, and he has not yet been produced before a magistrate.

These facts indicate a clear violation of the procedural safeguards established in *Bangladesh & ors Vs. BLAST & ors*. The police are legally obligated to have informed a relative or nominated friend within 12 hours of arrest and recorded all relevant details in the diary, as above. Their failure to do so is unlawful.

Additionally, under Section 54 of the Code of Criminal Procedure (not excerpted here but referenced in the judgment), no person may be detained beyond 24 hours without being produced before a magistrate — which has also been violated, as the arrest occurred on 10 May and as of 14 May, no production has taken place.

The family should immediately file an application before the High Court Division under Article 102 of the Constitution for habeas corpus or to enforce fundamental rights, citing these violations.

According to the Appellate Division’s guidelines in *Bangladesh & ors Vs. BLAST & ors*, 8 SCOB [2016] AD 1, the police are required to:

(i) Prepare a memorandum of arrest at the time of arrest and obtain the signature of the arrestee with the date and time of arrest;  
(ii) Intimate a nearest relative of the arrestee — or, in the absence of a relative, a friend suggested by the arrestee — as soon as practicable but not later than six hours after the arrest, notifying them of the time, place of arrest, and place of custody;  
(iii) Make an entry in the police diary recording the grounds of arrest, the name and address of the person who informed or complained about the arrest, and disclose the names and particulars of the relative or friend to whom information was given;  
(iv) Send copies of all relevant documents — including the memorandum of arrest, a copy of the information or complaint relating to the cognizable offence, and entries in the diary — to the Magistrate at the time of production of the arrestee under Section 167 of the Code of Criminal Procedure.

In this case, the police failed to inform the family of Md. Asif Hossain’s arrest for over 24 hours (until the next afternoon), and no information was provided about his location or the reason for detention — which violates the mandatory procedural safeguards laid down by the Appellate Division in *Bangladesh & ors Vs. BLAST & ors*. Additionally, the failure to produce him before a Magistrate within 24 hours (as required under Section 167 of the Code of Criminal Procedure) further compounds the illegality.

These requirements are binding and non-negotiable, even if no warrant was issued — as arrest without warrant is permitted under Section 100 of the Dhaka Metropolitan Police Ordinance, 1976, but not exempt from these procedural safeguards.

Under the Bangladesh EPZ Labour Act, 2019 (Act No. 2 of 2019), a female worker is entitled to maternity benefit for a period of eight (8) weeks immediately following childbirth (Section 29(1)). Additionally, she is entitled to receive this maternity benefit for the period of eight (8) weeks preceding the expected date of delivery and eight (8) weeks following childbirth, provided she has worked for at least six (6) months in the establishment prior to the expected delivery (Section 30(1)).

The law explicitly prohibits an employer from requiring a female worker to work during the eight (8) weeks after childbirth (Section 29(1)), and it further states that no notice or order dismissing, discharging, or removing a female worker during the period of six (6) months before and eight (8) weeks after childbirth shall deprive her of her maternity benefit unless there is sufficient cause for such action (Section 34). This protection is mirrored in the Bangladesh Labour Act, 2006 (Act No. 42 of 2006), Section 50.

The company’s policy granting only four (4) weeks of unpaid leave directly contravenes these statutory provisions. The law mandates eight (8) weeks of paid maternity benefit before and after childbirth — not four weeks, and certainly not unpaid. Furthermore, the requirement to rejoin on 18 May 2026 (only six weeks post-delivery) is unlawful, as it falls short of the mandatory eight-week post-natal leave period.

The employer’s assertion that “no maternity benefit will be paid as per company policy” is invalid. No company policy can override statutory rights under Sections 29 and 30 of the Bangladesh EPZ Labour Act, 2019 or Section 45 of the Bangladesh Labour Act, 2006.

You are legally entitled to:
- Eight (8) weeks of paid maternity leave before delivery;
- Eight (8) weeks of paid maternity leave after delivery;
- Protection from dismissal during the period of six months prior to and eight weeks after childbirth (Section 34);
- No requirement to return to work before the expiry of the full post-natal eight-week period.

The company’s notice is unlawful, and you may seek redress through the Labour Court under the relevant provisions.
